From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:52:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 167/171] cachefile: the content of a remote rule set in a file
 of its own

In bbolt a list of megabytes took three times its size or more: a changed
value is written anew, the freed pages stay in the file and are never given
back. Measured with a million subnets and 800000 names (34 MB of lists):
cache.db of 106 MB after the first update and 141 MB after five, on flash or
in RAM where the cache file is. Each save also held two more copies of the
list in memory (the marshalled value and the pages written).

The content now goes into <cache path>.rule-sets/<tag>-<hash>, written and
renamed into place; the bucket rule_set_file keeps the rest and the size,
and a file of another size (a write cut by a power loss) is downloaded
again. A record of an earlier version is read until the next save, which
deletes it; an earlier version finds no record and downloads the list. A
start removes the files no record names.
